       (4, 500.00, '2023-04-20');
🧑‍💻 Join Results
🔗 INNER JOIN
sql
SELECT c.name, o.order_id, o.amount
FROM customers c
INNER JOIN orders o
ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id;
Result

name	order_id	amount
Alice	1	250.00
Alice	2	300.00
Bob	3	150.00
Diana	4	500.00
👉 Only customers with orders appear. Charlie is excluded.

🔗 LEFT JOIN
sql
SELECT c.name, o.order_id, o.amount
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o
ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id;
Result

name	order_id	amount
Alice	1	250.00
Alice	2	300.00
Bob	3	150.00
Charlie	NULL	NULL
Diana	4	500.00
👉 All customers appear. Charlie shows up with NULL because he has no orders.

🔗 RIGHT JOIN
sql
SELECT c.name, o.order_id, o.amount
FROM customers c
RIGHT JOIN orders o
ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id;
Result

name	order_id	amount
Alice	1	250.00
Alice	2	300.00
Bob	3	150.00
Diana	4	500.00
👉 All orders appear. If there were an order without a matching customer, the name would be NULL.

✅ Final Summary
INNER JOIN → only matched rows.

LEFT JOIN → all from left + matches.

RIGHT JOIN → all from right + matches.

FULL OUTER JOIN → workaround using UNION.
